# Simmers et al. - DHO: Health Science.
# Chapter list: 10th edition (2026), from Cengage/National Geographic Learning's product data (ngl.cengage.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

CHAPTERS = [
    (1, "History and Trends of Health Care", [
        "Health care has changed dramatically over time. Ancient Egyptians kept the first medical records and used herbs; ancient Greeks, led by Hippocrates (the 'father of medicine'), based medicine on observation rather than religion; and the Romans built public health systems such as aqueducts and sewers. During the Middle Ages, religion dominated and epidemics like the bubonic plague killed millions. The Renaissance brought anatomy studies by Vesalius and Leonardo da Vinci.",
        "Key discoveries include the microscope (Leeuwenhoek), circulation of blood (William Harvey), the smallpox vaccine (Edward Jenner, 1796), germ theory and pasteurization (Louis Pasteur), antiseptic surgery (Joseph Lister), X-rays (Wilhelm Roentgen), penicillin (Alexander Fleming, 1928), and the polio vaccine (Jonas Salk). Florence Nightingale founded modern nursing, and Clara Barton founded the American Red Cross. The 20th and 21st centuries brought organ transplants, DNA discoveries, the Human Genome Project, and genetic engineering.",
        "Current trends include cost containment (controlling rising costs through methods like diagnostic-related groups, combined services, outpatient care, and mass purchasing), home health care, geriatric care for an aging population, telemedicine and telehealth, wellness and holistic health, complementary and alternative therapies, and preparing for bioterrorism and pandemics like COVID-19. Electronic health records and the Affordable Care Act have also reshaped care.",
    ], [
        "Hippocrates = father of medicine",
        "Jenner = smallpox vaccine; Pasteur = germ theory; Lister = antiseptics",
        "Fleming = penicillin; Salk = polio vaccine",
        "Nightingale = modern nursing; Barton = American Red Cross",
        "Trends: cost containment, home care, telehealth, wellness",
    ]),
    (2, "Health Care Systems", [
        "Health care facilities include hospitals, long-term care facilities, medical and dental offices, clinics, urgent care centers, home health agencies, hospice (care for the terminally ill), mental health facilities, genetic counseling centers, rehabilitation facilities, health maintenance organizations, industrial and school health services, and public health services. Government agencies include the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, the CDC, FDA, NIH, and OSHA. Voluntary (nonprofit) agencies such as the American Heart Association are funded by donations.",
        "Health care is paid for through insurance plans (premiums, deductibles, and co-payments), managed care such as HMOs and PPOs, Medicare (for people 65 and older or with certain disabilities), Medicaid (for low-income people, run jointly by states and the federal government), workers' compensation, TRICARE (for military families), and the Children's Health Insurance Program. The Affordable Care Act of 2010 expanded coverage.",
        "Organizational structure shows lines of authority from the board of directors down through departments. Patients have rights, including the right to considerate care, information, refusal of treatment, and privacy, as described in patient bills of rights. Facilities can be accredited by groups such as The Joint Commission.",
    ], [
        "Hospice = care for terminally ill patients",
        "Medicare = 65+; Medicaid = low income",
        "HMO = managed care with a primary care gatekeeper",
        "CDC, FDA, NIH, OSHA are government agencies",
        "Premium, deductible, co-payment",
    ]),
    (3, "Careers in Health Care", [
        "Health careers require different levels of education: a certificate or diploma, an associate degree (2 years), a bachelor's degree (4 years), a master's degree, or a doctorate. Careers can also be described by level: professional, technologist or therapist, technician, and assistant or aide. Licensure is granted by a state government, certification by a professional association, and registration by a regulatory body. Continuing education units keep credentials current.",
        "The chapter surveys career clusters, including dental, diagnostic (lab, imaging), emergency medical services, health information, hospital/health care facility services, medical (physicians and physician assistants), mental and social services, mortuary, nursing, nutrition, therapeutic (physical, occupational, respiratory, speech therapy), veterinary, vision, and environmental and public health careers.",
        "Health care workers should match careers to their interests and abilities and consider entrepreneurship, job outlook, and work settings. The National Consortium for Health Science Education and the National Health Science Standards provide a framework for health science programs. Career and technical student organizations such as HOSA help students build leadership skills.",
    ], [
        "Licensure = state; certification = professional association",
        "Associate = 2 years; bachelor's = 4 years",
        "Levels: professional, technologist/therapist, technician, assistant",
        "CEUs keep credentials current",
        "HOSA is a CTSO for future health professionals",
    ]),
    (4, "Personal & Professional Qualities of a Health Care Team Member", [
        "Health care workers must present a professional appearance: good personal hygiene, clean and neat uniforms, minimal jewelry and makeup, short clean nails, hair worn off the collar, and good posture. Good health habits such as proper nutrition, exercise, rest, and avoiding tobacco and drugs help workers stay effective.",
        "Important personal qualities include empathy (understanding others' feelings), honesty, dependability, willingness to learn, patience, acceptance of criticism, enthusiasm, self-motivation, tact, competence, responsibility, discretion, and teamwork. Health care workers must stay within their scope of practice.",
        "Communication is a major focus. The communication process has a sender, a message, and a receiver, plus feedback. Barriers include hearing or vision loss, language differences, aphasia, and emotional states. Active listening, appropriate nonverbal communication, and accurate recording and reporting are essential. Teamwork, leadership styles (democratic, laissez-faire, autocratic), and managing stress and time are also covered.",
    ], [
        "Empathy = identifying with and understanding others' feelings",
        "Communication: sender, message, receiver, feedback",
        "Barriers: hearing loss, language, aphasia, emotions",
        "Leadership: democratic, laissez-faire, autocratic",
        "Stay within your scope of practice",
    ]),
    (5, "Legal and Ethical Responsibilities", [
        "Legal responsibilities fall under criminal law (wrongs against the public) and civil law (wrongs against a person). Torts include malpractice (professional negligence), negligence (failure to give expected care), assault (threat or attempt to injure), battery (unlawful touching without consent), invasion of privacy, false imprisonment (restraining without authority), abuse (physical, verbal, psychological, sexual), and defamation: slander (spoken) and libel (written).",
        "Contracts require an offer, acceptance, and consideration. Informed consent means the patient understands the procedure, risks, and alternatives before agreeing. Minors and mentally incompetent people need a parent or guardian. Agents act on behalf of employers. Medical records are legal documents and must be accurate, corrected properly, and kept confidential under HIPAA (the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act).",
        "Ethics are principles of right and wrong conduct, including euthanasia, abortion, genetic testing, and allocation of resources. Patients' rights, advance directives (living wills and durable power of attorney for health care), the Patient Self-Determination Act, and do-not-resuscitate (DNR) orders protect patient choices. Professional standards include working within your scope of practice, keeping confidentiality, and reporting errors.",
    ], [
        "Assault = threat; battery = unlawful touching",
        "Slander = spoken; libel = written",
        "Malpractice = professional negligence",
        "Informed consent explains risks, benefits, alternatives",
        "Living will + durable power of attorney = advance directives",
    ]),
    (6, "Medical Terminology", [
        "Medical terms are built from prefixes, word roots, and suffixes. The prefix comes before the root (hyper- = above, hypo- = below), the root carries the main meaning, and the suffix comes at the end (-itis = inflammation, -ectomy = surgical removal, -ology = study of). Combining vowels (usually 'o') link roots to suffixes that begin with consonants.",
        "Health care workers also use many abbreviations, such as BP (blood pressure), NPO (nothing by mouth), stat (immediately), bid (twice a day), and prn (as needed). The Joint Commission's 'Do Not Use' list bans error-prone abbreviations such as U for unit and qd for daily.",
        "Learning word parts lets workers interpret unfamiliar terms: gastroenterology is the study of the stomach and intestines; tachycardia is a fast heart rate; arthritis is joint inflammation. Accurate spelling and pronunciation prevent errors.",
    ], [
        "Prefix + root + suffix",
        "hyper- above/excess; hypo- below/deficient",
        "-ectomy removal; -otomy incision; -ostomy opening",
        "NPO nothing by mouth; stat immediately; prn as needed",
        "Avoid 'Do Not Use' abbreviations like U and qd",
    ]),
    (7, "Anatomy and Physiology", [
        "Anatomy is the study of body structure; physiology is the study of body function. The body is organized into cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Cells contain a nucleus (with DNA), cytoplasm, and organelles such as mitochondria (energy). Cells divide by mitosis. The four tissue types are epithelial, connective, nervous, and muscle. Body planes, directions, and cavities describe locations.",
        "This long chapter covers every body system: integumentary (skin), skeletal (206 bones), muscular (skeletal, smooth, cardiac muscle), nervous (CNS and PNS), special senses, circulatory (heart and blood vessels), blood, lymphatic, respiratory, digestive, urinary, endocrine, and reproductive systems. For each system it explains the structures, how they work, and common diseases and disorders.",
        "Examples of disorders include osteoporosis and fractures (skeletal), muscular dystrophy (muscular), stroke and multiple sclerosis (nervous), cataracts and glaucoma (eyes), atherosclerosis and myocardial infarction (circulatory), anemia and leukemia (blood), asthma and COPD (respiratory), hepatitis and ulcers (digestive), kidney stones and renal failure (urinary), diabetes mellitus (endocrine), and sexually transmitted infections (reproductive).",
    ], [
        "Anatomy = structure; physiology = function",
        "Mitochondria produce energy; nucleus holds DNA",
        "Adult skeleton = 206 bones",
        "CNS = brain + spinal cord",
        "Each system: structures, functions, diseases",
    ]),
    (8, "Human Growth and Development", [
        "Development is divided into life stages: infancy (birth to 1 year, the most rapid growth), early childhood (1–6), late childhood (6–12), adolescence (12–20, puberty), early adulthood (20–40), middle adulthood (40–65), and late adulthood (65 and older). Each stage has physical, mental, emotional, and social changes.",
        "Erik Erikson described eight psychosocial stages, such as trust vs. mistrust in infancy and identity vs. role confusion in adolescence. Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs says people meet physiological needs (food, water, oxygen) first, then safety, love and belonging, esteem, and finally self-actualization.",
        "Elisabeth Kübler-Ross described five stages of grieving for the dying: denial, anger, bargaining, depression, and acceptance. Defense mechanisms (such as denial, rationalization, projection, and repression) help people cope with stress. Hospice and palliative care support the dying and their families.",
    ], [
        "Infancy = fastest growth",
        "Maslow: physiological → safety → love → esteem → self-actualization",
        "Erikson: 8 psychosocial stages",
        "Kübler-Ross: denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance",
        "Defense mechanisms reduce anxiety",
    ]),
    (9, "Geriatric Care", [
        "Geriatrics is care of the elderly; gerontology is the study of aging. The elderly population is growing quickly. Myths about aging, such as 'all elderly people are senile,' are untrue; most older adults are independent.",
        "Physical changes include thinner, drier skin; brittle bones; slower reflexes; decreased vision (presbyopia, cataracts) and hearing (presbycusis); reduced lung capacity; slower digestion; and more frequent urination. Health care workers should promote safety, encourage activity, and help with communication by speaking clearly and facing the person.",
        "Confusion and disorientation can result from cerebral arteriosclerosis, strokes, or dementias such as Alzheimer disease. Reality orientation helps confused patients. Psychosocial changes include retirement, loss of a spouse, and changes in living situation. Meeting the needs of the elderly includes respecting cultural differences and the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (OBRA) rights for residents in long-term care facilities.",
    ], [
        "Geriatrics = care; gerontology = study of aging",
        "Most older adults are not senile",
        "Presbyopia = vision; presbycusis = hearing",
        "Reality orientation helps confused patients",
        "OBRA protects long-term care residents",
    ]),
    (10, "Cultural Diversity", [
        "Culture is the values, beliefs, and practices shared by a group, passed from generation to generation. Ethnicity refers to a group's shared heritage and identity, and race usually refers to physical characteristics. Health care workers should provide culturally competent care.",
        "Bias, prejudice, and stereotyping are barriers. A bias is a preference that inhibits impartial judgment, prejudice is a strong feeling or belief about a person or subject formed without knowledge, and stereotyping assumes all members of a group are alike. Workers should avoid all three.",
        "Cultural differences affect family organization, language, personal space and touch, eye contact, gestures, health care beliefs, spirituality and religion, and food preferences. Interpreters should be used when language differs. Respecting each person's beliefs, asking questions, and avoiding assumptions improve care.",
    ], [
        "Culture = shared values, beliefs, practices",
        "Bias, prejudice, stereotyping = barriers",
        "Use a professional interpreter for language differences",
        "Respect religious and dietary practices",
        "Culturally competent care treats each person as an individual",
    ]),
    (11, "Nutrition and Diets", [
        "Nutrition is how the body uses food. Good nutrition supports growth, energy, and disease prevention. The six essential nutrients are carbohydrates (main energy source), lipids (fats, concentrated energy), proteins (build and repair tissue), vitamins, minerals, and water.",
        "Fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E, and K; water-soluble vitamins are the B complex and C. Important minerals include calcium (bones), iron (hemoglobin), sodium and potassium (fluid balance). Metabolism converts nutrients into energy, measured in calories. MyPlate and the Dietary Guidelines for Americans guide healthy eating, and food labels show nutrients per serving.",
        "Therapeutic diets include the regular diet, clear liquid diet, full liquid diet, soft diet, diabetic diet, calorie-controlled diet, low-cholesterol diet, fat-restricted diet, sodium-restricted (low-salt) diet, protein diets, and bland diet. Disorders related to poor nutrition include obesity, anorexia nervosa, bulimia, and vitamin deficiencies such as scurvy (vitamin C) and rickets (vitamin D).",
    ], [
        "Six nutrients: carbs, lipids, proteins, vitamins, minerals, water",
        "Fat-soluble vitamins: A, D, E, K",
        "Carbohydrates = main energy source",
        "Clear liquid diet = transparent liquids like broth and gelatin",
        "Scurvy = vitamin C; rickets = vitamin D",
    ]),
    (12, "Computers and Technology in Health Care", [
        "Computers are used throughout health care for electronic health records (EHRs), scheduling, billing, insurance claims, inventory, and communication. EHRs improve access to information and reduce errors but must be protected.",
        "Diagnostic technology includes computerized tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), ultrasonography, and digital radiography. Treatment technology includes robotic surgery, computer-controlled infusion pumps, and medical devices. Telemedicine allows care at a distance, and patients use online portals and wearable monitors.",
        "Security and privacy are essential. HIPAA requires protecting patient information. Workers must use strong passwords, log off, avoid sharing information on social media, and follow facility policies. The chapter also covers internet research and evaluating the reliability of health information.",
    ], [
        "EHR = electronic health record",
        "CT, MRI, PET, ultrasound = computerized imaging",
        "Telemedicine = care at a distance",
        "HIPAA protects patient information",
        "Never share passwords or patient info on social media",
    ]),
]

CHAPTERS += [
    (13, "Medical Math", [
        "Health care workers use math for doses, measurements, and records. Basic skills include whole numbers, decimals, fractions, percentages, ratios, and proportions. Rounding and estimating help check answers.",
        "The metric system uses base units of meter (length), liter (volume), and gram (mass), with prefixes such as kilo- (1,000), centi- (1/100), milli- (1/1,000), and micro- (1/1,000,000). Common conversions include 1 kg = 2.2 lb, 1 inch = 2.54 cm, 1 teaspoon = 5 mL, 1 ounce = 30 mL, and 1 cup = 240 mL. Household and apothecary measurements are still sometimes used.",
        "Temperature conversions use °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32. Military (24-hour) time avoids AM/PM confusion: 1:00 PM is 1300 hours. Roman numerals (I, V, X, L, C, D, M) still appear in some settings.",
    ], [
        "kilo = 1,000; milli = 1/1,000",
        "1 kg = 2.2 lb; 1 in = 2.54 cm",
        "1 tsp = 5 mL; 1 oz = 30 mL",
        "°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9",
        "1:00 PM = 1300 hours",
    ]),
    (14, "Promotion of Safety", [
        "Body mechanics prevent injury: keep a broad base of support, bend at the hips and knees, keep the back straight, carry objects close to the body, and avoid twisting. Ergonomics adapts the environment to prevent strain.",
        "OSHA requires Safety Data Sheets (SDS) for hazardous chemicals, and the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard protects workers. Patient safety includes identifying patients correctly, keeping beds low, side rails as ordered, call signals within reach, and avoiding restraints unless ordered. Equipment and solutions must be used correctly.",
        "Fire requires heat, fuel, and oxygen (the fire triangle). In a fire, follow RACE: Rescue, Alarm, Contain, and Extinguish. Use PASS for extinguishers: Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side. Class A fires involve paper and wood, Class B flammable liquids, Class C electrical equipment, and Class D combustible metals. Facilities also plan for disasters and emergencies.",
    ], [
        "Bend at hips and knees; keep a broad base of support",
        "SDS = hazardous chemical information",
        "RACE: Rescue, Alarm, Contain, Extinguish",
        "PASS: Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep",
        "Class A ordinary, B liquids, C electrical, D metals",
    ]),
    (15, "Infection Control", [
        "Microorganisms include bacteria (cocci, bacilli, spirilla), viruses, fungi, protozoa, and rickettsiae. Pathogens cause disease; nonpathogens normally do not. The chain of infection has six links: causative agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, and susceptible host. Breaking any link stops the spread.",
        "Asepsis means freedom from disease-causing organisms. Antisepsis prevents or slows growth, disinfection destroys or kills most pathogens on objects, and sterilization (such as autoclaving) kills all microorganisms, including spores. Hand hygiene is the most important way to prevent infection. Healthcare-associated (nosocomial) infections are acquired in a facility.",
        "Standard precautions treat all blood and body fluids as potentially infectious. Transmission-based precautions add protection: airborne (e.g., tuberculosis, requiring a respirator and special room), droplet (e.g., influenza, requiring a mask within 3 feet), and contact (e.g., MRSA, requiring gloves and gown). Personal protective equipment (PPE), sterile technique, and protective isolation are also taught.",
    ], [
        "Chain of infection: agent, reservoir, exit, transmission, entry, host",
        "Sterilization kills all organisms including spores",
        "Hand hygiene = most important infection control",
        "Standard precautions for all patients",
        "Airborne, droplet, contact precautions",
    ]),
    (16, "Vital Signs", [
        "Vital signs are temperature, pulse, respiration, and blood pressure; pain is often called the fifth vital sign, and oxygen saturation is also measured. Normal oral temperature is about 98.6°F (37°C); rectal readings are about 1 degree higher and axillary about 1 degree lower. Temperatures can also be taken tympanically or temporally.",
        "The adult pulse is normally 60–100 beats per minute, most often taken at the radial artery. Tachycardia is over 100, bradycardia under 60. An apical pulse is taken with a stethoscope over the heart's apex, and an apical-radial deficit is the difference between the two. Normal adult respirations are 12–20 per minute; dyspnea is difficult breathing, apnea is no breathing, and Cheyne-Stokes is a pattern of periods of apnea and deep, rapid breathing.",
        "Blood pressure is the force of blood against artery walls: systolic (heart contracting) over diastolic (heart relaxing). Normal is less than 120/80 mmHg, and hypertension is 130/80 or higher. It's measured with a sphygmomanometer and stethoscope. Accurate recording and reporting of abnormal readings is essential.",
    ], [
        "Normal oral temp ≈ 98.6°F (37°C)",
        "Adult pulse 60–100; respirations 12–20",
        "Tachycardia > 100; bradycardia < 60",
        "Normal BP < 120/80; hypertension ≥ 130/80",
        "Sphygmomanometer measures blood pressure",
    ]),
    (17, "First Aid", [
        "First aid is immediate care given to an injured or ill person before professional help arrives. First check the scene for safety, check the victim, call emergency services, and then provide care. Good Samaritan laws protect people who help in good faith. Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) and AED use follow current guidelines.",
        "Bleeding is controlled with direct pressure and, if needed, a tourniquet. Shock (inadequate blood flow) causes pale, cool, clammy skin and rapid pulse; keep the victim lying down and warm. Poisoning calls for contacting Poison Control. Burns are classified as superficial (first degree), partial-thickness (second degree), and full-thickness (third degree) and are cooled with water.",
        "Other emergencies include heat cramps, heat exhaustion, and heat stroke (a medical emergency); hypothermia and frostbite; fractures, dislocations, sprains, and strains (immobilize the injury); sudden illness such as heart attack, stroke, diabetic emergencies, and seizures; and injuries to the eyes, head, chest, and abdomen. Dressings cover wounds; bandages hold dressings in place.",
    ], [
        "Check, call, care",
        "Direct pressure stops most bleeding",
        "Shock: pale, cool, clammy; keep warm and lying down",
        "Heat stroke = emergency; body temp very high",
        "Dressing covers a wound; bandage holds it",
    ]),
    (18, "Preparing for the World of Work", [
        "Job-keeping skills include dependability, a positive attitude, following rules, and continuing to learn. Employers look for both technical skills and soft skills such as communication and teamwork.",
        "A cover letter introduces you; a résumé summarizes your education, experience, and skills; and a portfolio shows your work. Job applications must be completed neatly and honestly. In an interview, dress professionally, arrive early, make eye contact, answer clearly, ask questions, and send a thank-you note afterward.",
        "Workers must understand their paycheck, including gross pay, deductions (taxes, Social Security, insurance), and net pay, and how to create a budget. Leaving a job properly with written notice keeps references positive.",
    ], [
        "Résumé summarizes education, experience, skills",
        "Cover letter introduces you to the employer",
        "Send a thank-you note after an interview",
        "Gross pay − deductions = net pay",
        "Give written notice when leaving a job",
    ]),
    (19, "Dental Assistant Skills", [
        "Dental assistants help the dentist during procedures, prepare patients, sterilize instruments, take radiographs, and mix materials. The adult mouth has 32 permanent teeth; children have 20 primary (deciduous) teeth. Teeth are identified by numbering systems such as the Universal system.",
        "Tooth anatomy includes enamel (hardest substance in the body), dentin, pulp (nerves and blood vessels), cementum, and the periodontal ligament. Tooth surfaces include mesial, distal, buccal, lingual, and occlusal. Common dental instruments include mirrors, explorers, cotton pliers, and excavators.",
        "Skills include charting conditions, positioning the patient, passing instruments, operating the oral evacuator, mixing cements, preparing alginate impressions, pouring models, and following infection control in the operatory.",
    ], [
        "32 permanent teeth; 20 primary teeth",
        "Enamel = hardest substance in the body",
        "Pulp contains nerves and blood vessels",
        "Mesial toward midline; distal away",
        "Alginate = impression material",
    ]),
    (20, "Laboratory Assistant Skills", [
        "Laboratory assistants collect and process specimens and perform basic tests. Safety includes using PPE, handling specimens as infectious, and labeling correctly. The microscope's parts include the eyepiece, objective lenses, stage, and light source; the oil immersion lens provides the highest magnification.",
        "Skills include capillary puncture, venipuncture (often in the median cubital vein), and the order of draw for blood tubes. Tests include hematocrit, hemoglobin, blood typing (ABO and Rh), blood glucose, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate.",
        "Urinalysis includes physical examination (color, clarity, specific gravity), chemical testing with reagent strips (glucose, protein, ketones, blood), and microscopic examination. Other skills include obtaining throat cultures and preparing slides.",
    ], [
        "Oil immersion = highest magnification",
        "Median cubital vein = common venipuncture site",
        "Hematocrit = % of red blood cells",
        "ABO and Rh blood typing",
        "Urinalysis: physical, chemical, microscopic",
    ]),
    (21, "Medical Assistant Skills", [
        "Medical assistants perform both administrative tasks (scheduling, records, billing) and clinical tasks (preparing patients, assisting with exams, giving injections where allowed). Accurate height and weight measurements and patient histories are basic skills.",
        "Patients are positioned for exams: horizontal recumbent (supine), prone, Sims' (left side), knee-chest, Fowler's (sitting, head raised), lithotomy, and Trendelenburg. Instruments include the otoscope, ophthalmoscope, and reflex hammer.",
        "Other skills include assisting with minor surgery, removing sutures, recording an electrocardiogram (ECG), performing vision screening with a Snellen chart, and following medication administration rules (the rights of medication administration: right patient, drug, dose, route, time, and documentation).",
    ], [
        "Fowler's = sitting, head of bed raised",
        "Sims' = left side lying",
        "Lithotomy = for pelvic exams",
        "Snellen chart tests distance vision",
        "Rights of medication administration",
    ]),
    (22, "Nurse Assistant Skills", [
        "Nurse assistants provide basic care under the supervision of nurses. Key skills include making occupied and unoccupied beds, positioning and turning patients, giving baths, oral hygiene, shaving, hair and nail care, and dressing patients.",
        "Moving patients safely uses a gait belt (transfer belt), proper body mechanics, and devices such as mechanical lifts. Range-of-motion exercises prevent contractures. Pressure injuries (bedsores) are prevented by turning patients at least every 2 hours and keeping skin clean and dry.",
        "Other skills include measuring intake and output, collecting specimens, caring for catheters, assisting with eating, applying restraints only as ordered, and providing postmortem care. Observing and reporting changes is a key responsibility.",
    ], [
        "Turn patients at least every 2 hours",
        "Gait belt for safe transfers",
        "Range-of-motion exercises prevent contractures",
        "Measure intake and output",
        "Report changes in condition",
    ]),
    (23, "Physical Therapy Skills", [
        "Physical therapy helps patients regain movement and function. Skills include performing range-of-motion exercises (active, passive, active-assistive), applying heat and cold treatments, and helping with ambulation.",
        "Ambulation devices include gait belts, crutches, canes, and walkers. Crutch gaits include the four-point, three-point, two-point, swing-to, and swing-through gaits. Canes are held on the strong side; walkers are moved forward before stepping.",
        "Heat increases circulation and relaxes muscles, while cold reduces swelling and pain. Both require careful monitoring to prevent burns or tissue damage. Other modalities include hydrotherapy and electrical stimulation.",
    ], [
        "Passive ROM = therapist moves the joint",
        "Cane on the strong side",
        "Heat increases circulation; cold reduces swelling",
        "Crutch gaits: 4-point, 3-point, 2-point, swing-to, swing-through",
        "Gait belt for ambulation safety",
    ]),
    (24, "Business and Accounting Skills", [
        "Business and accounting skills support the administrative side of health care. They include filing (alphabetical, numerical, and subject systems), managing records, and using computers.",
        "Communication skills include answering the telephone professionally, scheduling appointments, and writing business letters and memos. Bookkeeping includes recording charges and payments, maintaining accounts receivable, writing receipts, and preparing statements.",
        "Workers also handle insurance forms, use medical codes (CPT for procedures and ICD for diagnoses), write checks, maintain petty cash, and keep accurate financial records.",
    ], [
        "Filing: alphabetical, numerical, subject",
        "CPT = procedure codes; ICD = diagnosis codes",
        "Accounts receivable = money owed to the business",
        "Answer phones professionally",
        "Keep accurate financial records",
    ]),
]

